H1("Abstract")
_REG=pd.read_csv("tables/abf_regret_vs_best_fixed.csv",index_col=0); _AB=pd.read_csv("tables/abf_summary.csv")
P(f"Health agencies can often test only some private wells for arsenic, so the order of testing matters. Machine-learning risk maps are the usual guide, but they are mostly scored with random splits. Using USGS results from US states held out one at a time, we simulated testing campaigns under a fixed budget. For arsenic ({nst} states), a random split gave ROC-AUC {rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.2f} and held-out states {rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.2f}. With 20% of a state's wells tested, a map trained on other states found {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times as many unsafe wells as random order (not significant after correction; worse than random in {len(bad_static)} states), and geologic, climate and elevation predictors did not change that materially. Re-ranking wells with the results of nearby wells tested earlier reached {s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order (about {tests_fb:.0f} tests per unsafe well found, against {tests_rand:.0f}), and a rule that only follows unsafe results did as well ({sw.H1_follow_unsafe.mean():.2f}). Gains were larger where unsafe wells cluster in space (Spearman ρ = {XM['as10'].loc['lift_20km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f}; confirmed in synthetic fields) but shrank when results arrived in large, late batches. Fluoride behaved differently: a map from other states did transfer ({S['f2'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f}) and a map-plus-feedback hybrid was best ({S['f2'].loc['M5_proposed','enr_20_mean']:.2f}). An adaptive Bayesian-mixture rule (ABF) that learns online how far to trust the map had the smallest mean ({_REG.loc['mean','ABF']:.2f}) and worst-case ({_REG.loc['worst_case','ABF']:.2f}) gap to the best fixed rule across six outcomes, with no tuning, but was not best in every outcome. A state's own earlier wells made the map useful, and history plus feedback was best on average ({hw.W6_history_prior_plus_feedback:.2f}). Uranium results were mixed and low-powered. The wells are not a random sample and no prospective test was done.")
P("Keywords: groundwater arsenic; private wells; spatial validation; adaptive sampling; screening; transferability",italic=True)
H1("1. Introduction")
P(f"Arsenic in drinking water harms health. Roughly 40 million people in the United States use private wells that fall outside public-supply regulation ({C('Methods and Uncertainty in Predictions')}); such wells are tested at the initiative of owners or health agencies and not through routine public-supply monitoring ({C('Reducing public exposure')}). Agencies therefore run outreach campaigns that must decide where to start, for example by ranking towns on well use and past water quality ({C('Private well ranking indices')}). Machine-learning maps estimate the chance that a private well exceeds an arsenic threshold ({C('Machine Learning Models of Arsenic in Private Wells','Machine-Learning Predictions of High Arsenic','Surface Variable')}). They are valuable for exposure research, but they are less clear as a guide for a campaign. The national private-well model of {CN('Machine Learning Models of Arsenic in Private Wells')} reported a sensitivity of 33.9% at 10 µg/L on its test data. Its test wells were a stratified 30% subset of all wells, and no spatial hold-out was described, so the figure says little about use in a state that contributed no data.")
P(f"Two questions follow. First, how well does a risk map work where it has not seen data? Spatially structured validation is studied in ecology and mapping ({C('Cross‐validation strategies','Spatial validation reveals','Predicting into unknown space')}) and in groundwater work ({C('Machine learning for predicting groundwater quality')}; {C('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')}), although the right way to judge map accuracy is debated ({C('Spatial cross-validation is not the right way')}). Second, a campaign is not a one-shot prediction. Results arrive while testing is under way, and nearby wells tend to share geology. Letters that cited a neighbour's exceedance made owners more likely to test ({C('Reducing public exposure')}), and risk-based sampling designs for arsenic exist ({C('Risk Based Arsenic')}). Active search for rare targets ({C('Bayesian Optimal Active Search')}; {C('Active pointillistic pattern search')}) and budgeted geospatial discovery ({C('Adapting Actively on the Fly')}) are related methods. We did not find a study that measures, on national data held out by state, how a map trained elsewhere compares with feedback from tested wells and with a state's own history.")
fig("figures/fig_story.png","Figure 1. The question studied here. An agency orders wells for testing, tests a batch, and may update the order from the results. Methods are scored by the unsafe wells found per 100 tests.",15)
P("We therefore simulate campaigns using the real arsenic results of USGS wells as the ground truth that testing would reveal. Our contributions are:")
for t in ("A campaign evaluation for well testing: methods are scored by tests needed per unsafe well found at a fixed budget, with states held out and a 25 km buffer.",
 f"A measure of how much random splits overstate map quality (ROC-AUC {rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.2f} against {rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.2f}; recall at 20% tested {rv.loc['random_5fold','recall_at_20']:.2f} against {rv.loc['leave_state_out','recall_at_20']:.2f}), and a test of whether geologic-map predictors close the gap.",
 "A comparison of static maps, a geology rule, feedback rules, a simple heuristic and hybrids across 11 methods and 5 seeds, with paired tests across states, and an analysis of why feedback works and when it fails (spatial clustering, weight on the map, regions, late results).",
 "A warm-start test, in which each state's earlier wells are available, showing when a map becomes useful.",
 "Negative results, reported in full: geology did not rescue the map, a hybrid of map and feedback did not beat feedback alone in a new state, and a simple heuristic matched the kernel rule.",
 "Two more contaminants: uranium (limited power) and fluoride, for which the answer differs from arsenic."):
    D.add_paragraph(t,style="List Number")
H1("2. Related work")
P(f"Mapping arsenic and related contaminants with machine learning is well developed. Global and national maps exist ({C('Global threat of arsenic')}; {C('Machine Learning Models of Arsenic in Private Wells')}), as do depth-specific maps ({C('Machine-Learning Predictions of High Arsenic')}), local models ({C('Revealing the sources of arsenic')}; {C('Anthropogenic and Geogenic')}) and models for fluoride and uranium ({C('high-fluoride groundwater in China')}; {C('Machine learning-based prediction of elevated uranium')}). This journal has published models of arsenic and fluoride ({C('Prediction of arsenic and fluoride')}), a review of machine learning in groundwater quality ({C('Application of machine learning in groundwater quality modeling')}), work on limited monitoring data ({C('Improving prediction of groundwater quality')}) and early warning for private systems ({C('Developing early warning systems')}). Some of the uranium models use chemistry measured in the water, which a planner does not have before testing ({C('Development of predictive equations')}; {C('Data driven prediction of uranium')}).")
P(f"Transfer to unsampled regions is the weak point. {CN('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')} found that fluoride models moved between basins only when predictors were hydrochemical and not surface variables, and that adding samples from the target region helped. {CN('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')} found that an environmental-only model had negligible national transferability under buffered spatial validation, and {CN('Machine learning for predicting groundwater quality')} stress buffered spatial validation for arsenic, nitrate and uranium. Transfer-learning methods aim to reduce the data needed ({C('Siamese')}). Our result on location-and-geology predictors agrees with these studies. What we add is a campaign view: the cost, in tests, of using a transferred map, and a comparison with feedback and local history.")
P(f"In practice, agencies target outreach by past results. Examples include free testing offers to owners whose earlier results fell between the old and new standards ({C('Targeted Private Well Outreach')}), events for at-risk wells ({C('Evaluating the impact of free private well testing')}) and town rankings ({C('Private well ranking indices')}). Retesting needs are studied separately ({C('Recommended sampling intervals')}).")
P(f"Sequential search with a budget is studied in machine learning. Active search seeks rare positive items with few queries ({C('Bayesian Optimal Active Search')}); pattern-search methods find regions of interest ({C('Active pointillistic pattern search')}); recent work targets under-observed high-risk regions under tight budgets ({C('Adapting Actively on the Fly')}) and balances exploiting and exploring in test-and-isolate problems ({C('Containing a spread')}). These works propose methods. We instead measure what simple rules achieve on real well data under held-out evaluation.")
H1("3. Data and methods")
H2("3.1 Data")
P(f"We downloaded discrete water-quality results from the USGS Samples API on 22–23 November 2026, one request per state ({C('USGS Samples Data API')}). For arsenic, the single-request download timed out for California and Colorado, so the main runs exclude them; a later date-window download retrieved them and they enter an extended run only. For uranium all states were retrieved. We kept results from wells, in water, in µg/L, and used the earliest valid sample per well. Non-detects were labelled safe, and non-detects whose detection limit was above the lower threshold were dropped. After filtering, arsenic had 53,294 wells in 47 states (9.97% above 10 µg/L; 17.3% above 5 µg/L); wells sharing coordinates were collapsed, leaving 50,139. Uranium had 28,117 wells in 49 states (5.1% above 30 µg/L; 13.6% above 10 µg/L). Fluoride (mg/L) had 150,299 wells in 48 states (Kansas could not be retrieved; 2.3% above 4 mg/L, the US limit; 6.5% above 2 mg/L). The base predictors are latitude, longitude and the USGS aquifer code (present for 42% of arsenic wells). Well depth was missing for every well and was not used. Geology predictors were added in a second step: each well was joined by location to the USGS State Geologic Map Compilation ({C('State Geologic Map Compilation')}), giving a generalized lithology class (28 classes) and a coarse geologic age.")
H2("3.2 Leakage audit and splits")
P("We collapsed wells that share coordinates (2,939 arsenic identifiers), used only the earliest sample per well, and used no result-derived predictor. The main split holds out one state at a time and removes from training every well within 25 km of the held-out wells. States need at least 100 wells and 15 unsafe wells to serve as test units. Eight states drawn at random before any model was run were used only for tuning (4 for uranium at 30 µg/L, where few states qualify). Fluoride runs used 3 seeds instead of 5 because of compute time. A second design clusters wells of evaluation states into 60 spatial blocks; a third keeps one well per 1 km cell to reduce clustered sampling.")
H2("3.3 Campaign simulation and scores")
P("In a held-out unit, wells are tested in batches of 10. Before testing, a method sees only the predictors; after each batch it sees the outcomes of the wells just tested. The score is the number of unsafe wells found when 5%, 10%, 20% and 30% of wells have been tested, divided by the number expected under random order. This ratio, the enrichment, is 1 for random order by construction. The primary measure is enrichment at 20%. We also report tests needed per unsafe well found, which is the reciprocal of unsafe wells found per test and is proportional to cost if every test costs the same.")
H2("3.4 Methods compared")
P(f"M1 is a static LightGBM map trained on all wells outside the buffer. M2 is a static logistic regression on coordinates. M3 is a static nearest-neighbour prevalence map. M4 ranks untested wells by a Gaussian-kernel weighted share of unsafe results among tested neighbours (first batch random). M5 combines a prior probability p from M1 with the kernel evidence: score = (κp + a)/(κ + a + b), where a and b are kernel-weighted counts of unsafe and safe results among tested wells and κ sets the weight kept by the prior. M6 samples from the matching Beta distribution. M7 refits LightGBM with the tested wells at fixed checkpoints. Four ablations remove the prior (A1), remove shrinkage (A2), fix the bandwidth (A3) or replace the prior with M3 (A4). Tuning used the same budget for all methods: 20 random-search trials for LightGBM and a 5 × 5 grid of bandwidth and κ on the tuning states only (selected for base predictors: {tun['h_kappa'][0]} km and κ = {tun['h_kappa'][1]}). Added baselines need no map: H1 follows unsafe results only (rank by kernel-weighted count of unsafe neighbours), H2 spreads tests evenly in space using no outcomes, and, in the geology runs, G0 ranks wells by the unsafe rate of the same lithology class in other states, a rule of the kind an expert could apply with a geologic map.")
H2("3.5 Added analyses")
P("To see why feedback works we measured, for each state, how strongly unsafe wells cluster: the chance that a well within r km of an unsafe well is also unsafe, divided by the state's share of unsafe wells (r = 2, 5, 20 km), and related it to feedback enrichment with Spearman correlation. We swept κ from 0.5 to 1000. In a warm-start design, the earliest half of each state's wells by sample date served as history and the campaign ran on the later half. A regional check compared the feedback gain across US Census regions, and a leave-one-region-out check recomputed the main comparison without each region. Two realism checks changed batch size (10, 50, 200 wells per round, to mimic late laboratory results) and hid 30% of wells (to mimic refusals or lack of access).")
H2("3.6 Statistics")
P(f"The unit of analysis is the held-out state (or block) after averaging seeds. We used paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests ({C('Individual Comparisons by Ranking Methods')}), the median paired difference with a bootstrap 95% confidence interval ({C('Bootstrap Methods')}; 10,000 resamples of units), and Holm correction within each family of comparisons. Pre-registered choices and every later deviation are in the supplementary files, with the reason for each and the date; deviations were logged before the runs they concern.")
H1("4. Results")
H2("4.1 Random splits overstate map quality")
table(pd.DataFrame({"Split":["Random 5-fold","Leave-one-state-out (25 km buffer)"],"ROC-AUC":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.3f}"],"PR-AUC":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_pr']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_pr']:.3f}"],"Recall at 20% tested":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','recall_at_20']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','recall_at_20']:.3f}"]}),"Table 1. The same LightGBM model under two evaluations (mean of 3 seeds; As > 10 µg/L; base predictors).")
fig("figures/fig_random_vs_blocked.png","Figure 2. Random splits versus held-out states for the same model.",13)
P("Under random splitting the map ranks unsafe wells well. Under held-out states the ranking is weak. About 10% of wells are unsafe, so a PR-AUC of 0.15 is only modestly above chance.")
H2("4.2 In a new state, a map from other states is close to random order")
P(f"At 20% of wells tested, the static map found {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times the unsafe wells of random order (95% CI {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_lo']:.2f}–{s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_hi']:.2f}). Against random order, the {cmp(X['as10'],'M1_static_lgbm','M0_random(=1)')}. It was below random order in {len(bad_static)} of {nst} states ({', '.join(bad_static)}). The logistic regression was no better than random ({s10.loc['M2_static_logreg','enr_20_mean']:.2f}). For the 5 µg/L threshold the static map was {S['as5'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order.")
H2("4.3 Geologic-map predictors help only some outcomes")
GE=pd.read_csv("tables/geology_effect.csv").set_index("outcome")
_dg=GE.loc['As>10','median_gain']
P(f"For arsenic above 10 µg/L, adding lithology and age moved the static map from {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} to {G.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} (95% CI {G.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_lo']:.2f}–{G.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_hi']:.2f}); the paired median gain was {_dg:+.2f} (Holm-adjusted p = {GE.loc['As>10','holm_p_gain']:.3f}) and the map was still not significantly better than random order (Holm-adjusted p = {row(X['as10_geo'],'M1_static_lgbm','M0_random(=1)').holm_p:.3f}). Its ROC-AUC under held-out states was {rvg.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.2f}, against {rvg.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.2f} for a random split. The expert-style lithology rule G0 reached {G.loc['G0_lithology_prevalence','enr_20_mean']:.2f}; against random order the {cmp(X['as10_geo'],'G0_lithology_prevalence','M0_random(=1)')}. Neighbour feedback with the same wells reached {G.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} and beat the lithology rule ({cmp(X['as10_geo'],'M4_neighbor_only','G0_lithology_prevalence')}). For the other outcomes, the paired median differences between the geology map and the base map were {GE.loc['As>5','median_gain']:+.2f} for arsenic above 5 µg/L, {GE.loc['U>30','median_gain']:+.2f} for uranium above 30 µg/L and {GE.loc['U>10','median_gain']:+.2f} for uranium above 10 µg/L; none of the four differences was significant after Holm correction (smallest adjusted p = {GE.holm_p_gain.min():.3f}). For both arsenic thresholds, feedback beat the geology-assisted map ({cmp(X['as5_geo'],'M4_neighbor_only','M1_static_lgbm')} for 5 µg/L). For uranium the two were not distinguishable with 9 and 12 states. The arsenic result agrees with findings that surface and environmental predictors transfer poorly ({C('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')}; {C('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')}). With so few uranium states we cannot say whether geology matters more for uranium than for arsenic.")
