P("The contaminant results show that the right weight on the map is not known in advance. ABF removes that choice. It keeps 31 experts: 10 map-free experts (spatial scale h = 2, 5, 10, 20, 50 km; prior weight κ = 0.5 or 2 on the state-wide share of unsafe wells), 20 map-based experts (the same h; κ = 0.5, 2, 10 or 50 on the map probability), and one map-only expert. Each expert predicts the chance that an untested well is unsafe as (κ·prior + a)/(κ + a + b), where a and b are kernel-weighted counts of unsafe and safe results among tested wells at scale h. After each batch the cumulative log loss of each expert on the wells just tested is updated before their results enter the evidence, and expert weights are proportional to exp(−cumulative log loss). Wells are ranked by the weighted mean prediction. There is no tuned parameter.")
P("Guarantee. Treat each expert as a probabilistic model that generates a batch of outcomes from earlier evidence. The mixture with weights proportional to exp(−cumulative log loss) is a Bayesian mixture of these models, so its cumulative joint log loss exceeds that of the best expert by at most ln N, with N = 31, which is 3.43 nats, for every outcome sequence and any number of batches. This is the standard mixture bound. It bounds prediction loss, not the number of unsafe wells found, and ranking uses the mixture's per-well predictions. In all runs the realised excess loss stayed within the bound (maximum "+f"{pd.read_csv('results/abf_as10/abf.csv').query('method==\"ABF\"').regret_nats.max():.2f} nats in arsenic above 10 µg/L).")
