H1("5. Discussion")
P("For an agency starting in a state with no data, the practical message is to plan for feedback and to get results back quickly. A rule as simple as ‘test near wells that just came back unsafe’ raised the number of unsafe wells found per test by about 80% over random order in our simulation, and for arsenic above 10 µg/L it beat a map trained on other states, with or without geologic predictors. Even-spread sampling did not help. Neighbour-based targeting is already used in outreach ("+C('Reducing public exposure')+"); our contribution is to measure its size against the maps that the literature produces, on national data held out by state.")
P(f"The map is not useless, but where it is trained matters. For arsenic above 10 µg/L a map trained on the state's own earlier wells ({hw.W3_map_local_history_only:.2f}) did better than one that also included other states' wells ({hw.W2_map_with_history:.2f}), and for 5 µg/L the two were similar ({wm5.W3_map_local_history_only.mean():.2f} and {wm5.W2_map_with_history.mean():.2f}). The best average result came from using local history as a prior and continuing to learn during the campaign. This fits findings from fluoride work, where adding samples from the target region improved transfer and surface predictors alone did not ({C('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')}). A plausible reading, which we did not test, is that arsenic depends on local chemistry and redox conditions that a model with location, climate and a coarse geologic map cannot learn from other states.")
P(f"The contaminant matters. For fluoride a map trained on other states carried real information and the hybrid was best on average, while for arsenic above 10 µg/L the hybrid was worse than feedback alone and for uranium the evidence was mixed. Our fluoride result sits between two published results: fluoride models moved between basins only with hydrochemical predictors ({C('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')}), and an environmental-only model had negligible national transfer ({C('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')}). Differences in geography, data and thresholds make direct comparison difficult. A fixed rule needs to know in advance whether the map transfers. ABF removes that need and had the smallest mean and worst-case gap to the best fixed rule here, but it is not the best rule in every outcome and it needs small testing rounds. A practical plan is to test whether a map transfers on held-out states when data allow, and otherwise to start with ABF or the follow-unsafe rule.")
P("The negative results are part of the finding. Geology and climate did not rescue the arsenic map at 10 µg/L, a transferred map made the hybrid worse for that outcome, and the simple heuristic matched the kernel rule. One possible reason, which we did not test, is that the prior carries regional patterns that do not transfer and the kernel rule corrects for this only slowly. The loss of advantage with large, late batches is a practical warning: feedback is only as good as the speed of laboratory turnaround. The planner (code/campaign_planner.py, available on request with the rest of the code) implements the follow-unsafe rule: it takes candidate wells, results so far and optional history, and returns the next batch. It is a research tool, not a validated public-health product.")
